{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: UT-N4 asserts the constant against its own definition — near-tautological**

```dart
test('UT-N4: the nudge text carries the truncation + continuation ask', () {
  expect(emptyContinuationNudge, contains('empty'));
  expect(emptyContinuationNudge, contains('truncated'));
  expect(emptyContinuationNudge, contains('continue the task'));
});
```

`emptyContinuationNudge` is defined two hundred lines up in the same PR with exactly these words, so this can only fail if someone deletes the words from the constant — and even then both the injector and the classifier use the same constant, so nothing breaks. As a regression guard it has no power.

Either drop it, or give it real teeth by pinning the exact full string:

```dart
test('UT-N4: the nudge text is pinned (exact-match classifier depends on it)', () {
  expect(
    emptyContinuationNudge,
    'your last reply was empty/truncated — continue the task',
  );
});
```

That at least makes any wording change a deliberate, reviewed event (the string is user-visible model context in every nudged retry).
